WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.620
GREG KUELGEN: Hello, my
name is Greg Kuelgen.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.305
I'm an architect with the
Apigee team at Google Cloud.

00:00:10.305 --> 00:00:12.240
I'm very excited
to be here today

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.252
with my friend and
colleague Debora Elkin, who

00:00:14.252 --> 00:00:15.960
will join us a little
later on to give us

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.858
an amazing demo of some
of this functionality

00:00:17.858 --> 00:00:18.900
that we talk about today.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.702
So let's get into it.

00:00:23.702 --> 00:00:25.830
I'm not going to read
this agenda slide,

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.670
but there's a key takeaway.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.880
How you can use Anthos
and Apigee as a means

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.670
for deploying secure APIs
while allowing developers

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:36.720
to focus on delivering
business value.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.960
Let's set this up
just a little bit.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.640
At my company, there is
an App Innovation Day.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.970
I built a killer app,
Jellybean Auto Count.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.710
We all want to win jelly
bean guessing contests.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:53.710
The app I built utilized some
sick machine learning models

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.510
to quickly and
accurately provide

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.520
a count of the jelly
beans in any jar

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.622
just by processing a photo.

00:00:59.622 --> 00:01:01.330
Everyone agreed that
this app was amazing

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.370
and we needed to make it
available to all our employees.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:08.370
I've got this great code,
but how should I deploy it?

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.180
I went to my
colleague Maria, as I

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.520
knew she was a wizard
on our ops team.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.358
Maria said, I have
just the thing.

00:01:14.358 --> 00:01:16.150
We've been doing
Kubernetes for a while now

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.108
and had learned that it
was difficult to manage

00:01:18.108 --> 00:01:21.550
multiple clusters, particularly
across different regions.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.920
There was a new technology
that the company was trying

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.320
called Anthos GKE.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.630
It promised to simplify
management of Kubernetes,

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.440
creating a consistent
experience for developers

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.375
while doing so securely.

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:36.430
It sounded fantastic to me,
so we went ahead and deployed

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:37.920
my app to Kubernetes.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.640
My app was pretty basic.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.130
But outside of following
secure coding practices,

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:45.010
I didn't have to concern myself
with security, scaling, et

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:45.520
cetera.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.590
People loved the app.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.940
Usage was through the roof.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.207
I used this as an opportunity
to recruit some team members.

00:01:54.207 --> 00:01:56.040
We were getting lots
of enhancement requests

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.860
and began working
through the backlog.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.410
Since our architecture was
based on microservices,

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.290
we saw a proliferation
of services.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.150
We quickly realized
that we needed a way

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.150
to help us manage and secure
all of these services.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.490
Back to Maria.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:11.990
Maria had an answer again.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:13.690
There is a reason I
keep going to her.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.060
In this case, we can
use a service mesh

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.870
to help us secure the services,
understand the interactions,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.540
and help us generally
manage the traffic.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.880
The great news is that we
have a fully managed service

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.670
mesh offering already
available as part of Anthos

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.767
called Anthos Service Mesh.

00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:32.350
This was, again, a great relief,
because we had enough backlog

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.892
to get through without worrying
about managing all of the TLS

00:02:34.892 --> 00:02:37.930
and certificates and
access control and routing,

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.090
and just all of that junk.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.880
As you can see, we simply
layered in Anthos Service Mesh

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.460
and kept going on our merry way.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:48.100
With just a little help from us,
the operators on Maria's team

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.410
added all of the policies
into the Anthos Service Mesh

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:54.190
to secure the communication
between all of our services.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.830
All of a sudden, other
teams in the enterprise

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.930
caught wind and wanted
access to these services

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.100
we were building.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.130
Before now, it was
just a couple of us,

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:04.393
and we all worked
very closely together.

00:03:04.393 --> 00:03:06.310
We wanted to let other
teams use our services,

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.560
but we didn't want to
take a bunch of our time

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.180
onboarding these teams.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.910
Back to Maria.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.770
I'm sure no one is
surprised to learn

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:16.270
that she had another solution.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.010
This time, Apigee hybrid.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.010
It runs on top of Anthos
GKE, and the runtime

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.990
can be co-located
with our services.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:26.200
It provides a way for us to make
our documentation discoverable

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.910
and allow internal
teams to gain access

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.230
to our APIs via self-service.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.960
No need to talk to
anyone on my team.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.700
So as you can see,
we layered in Apigee

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.160
to help manage
these APIs that we

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.630
were exposing to other teams.

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:39.950
More success.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.500
At this point, Teresa, one
of the execs took notice.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.330
She saw the
popularity of this app

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.830
and knew we had to
make it available

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:47.350
to external developers.

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.100
Turns out we already
had all of the pieces

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.610
in place to make that happen.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.120
We simply needed to add a
few more Apigee capabilities,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.750
such as threat protection,
rate limiting, and OIDC.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.173
We also needed to ensure that
we could handle the load.

00:03:59.173 --> 00:04:00.590
We simply deployed
another cluster

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:06.590
of GKE and Apigee hybrid in GCP
to ensure that we could scale.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:08.120
So that's all great.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.090
But as I stated,
I'm just a developer

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:11.900
that wants to deliver
business value.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.380
I don't know anything about
exposing APIs to third parties.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.707
How can this be made as
simple as possible for me?

00:04:16.707 --> 00:04:18.790
With that, I'm going to
turn things over to Debora

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.010
to walk us through
how we might be

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.620
able to enable someone like me.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:24.820
DEBORA ELKIN: Thanks, Greg.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.785
Let's explore this with a demo.

00:04:26.785 --> 00:04:29.850
In the demo, we will
see how we can leverage

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:33.850
CI/CD pipelines to make it
very easy to expose services

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.530
through secure APIs.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:39.400
It is standard practice
when working with services

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.500
to use CI/CD pipelines to
automatically build and deploy

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.120
whenever changes are committed.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.630
This not only increases
developer productivity

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.880
and software quality,
but also makes

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:52.730
it easy to ensure best
practices are followed

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.655
when developing services.

00:04:54.655 --> 00:04:59.890
In the demo, the CI/CD pipeline
is governed by Cloud Build.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:02.920
When new code is checked
into a repository managed

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.090
by Cloud Source
Repositories, Cloud Build

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.960
triggers a new build.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.050
This will generate a
new container image,

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.360
check it into Cloud
Container Registry,

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.720
and deploy this container
into a service cluster.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:21.200
We can use a similar approach
for making it very easy

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.530
to build an API from a service.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:28.640
When the service developer
tags the source code,

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:32.390
Cloud Builder will orchestrate
the generation of a new API

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:37.040
proxy and deploy it to
an Apigee hybrid runtime.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.820
Cloud Builder will test the
API is successfully working

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:44.210
and check the newly generated
API configuration back

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.760
into Cloud Source Repository.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.550
Even without having a deep
knowledge of API development,

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.060
a service developer
can easily generate

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:56.540
an API that incorporates
the best practices adopted

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:57.980
by their company.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:02.240
Once the API is deployed,
clients can access this API.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.450
All external access
is mediated by Apigee,

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.330
providing a secure layer
and eliminating the need

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.900
to expose the Service
Cluster externally.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:14.770
The demo we are going
to see is from the point

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.350
of view of a service
developer, well

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:19.540
versed in software
development, but not

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:23.340
necessarily an expert
in API management.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:27.570
Cloud Source Repository is
useful code source control.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:31.740
Typically, the developer
will also use a local editor

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:35.830
on their workstation to
work on the source code,

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:39.240
check to their local
repository, and push the changes

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.510
to the remote repository.

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:46.440
Cloud Container Registry is
used to store all the container

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:51.050
images that are generated
during the development process.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:55.580
The services are deployed to
an Anthos [? ASM ?] cluster.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:58.580
In this case, it is a
development cluster.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.280
Additionally, there's
another GKE cluster

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.040
in which an instance of
Apigee hybrid runtime

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.190
has been deployed.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:09.470
Let's have a look in
Cloud Source Repository.

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:12.340
In mature teams, the
structure of the repository

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:16.000
is generated automatically
when the project is created,

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.060
and it [INAUDIBLE] templates
that encourage best practices

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:20.590
in software development.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.570
It even enforces them sometimes.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:27.460
A very common practice is to
have CI/CD pipelines-- that

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:30.160
is Continuous Integration,
Continued Development

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.760
pipelines-- that are triggered
when code is checked.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.670
The pipeline triggers the
build of a new container image

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.890
and deployment of
this newly built image

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:39.895
into a service cluster.

00:07:43.351 --> 00:07:46.090
Let's see this in action.

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:50.610
The developer will work on
their local visual editor.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:53.540
They will make
changes to their code.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:56.200
And once they are ready,
they will commit it

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.270
to their local repository.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:10.220
They will then
push those changes

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:11.960
to the remote repository.

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:18.100
When the changes reach
the remote repository,

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.260
a new build is started.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:24.490
This is managed by Cloud Build.

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:27.265
Let's have a look at what
happens during the build.

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:36.390
The first step builds
the new container image.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:40.064
It then pushes it to
container registry.

00:08:43.139 --> 00:08:46.955
After that, it is deployed
to the Anthos Cluster.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:53.780
The last step verifies
that all the artifacts

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:56.510
have been deployed and that
they are successfully working.

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:05.500
Let's go back to Cloud
Source Repositories

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:08.140
and have a look in more
detail to the structure

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.450
of the repository.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:14.400
We can see that there
is an API folder.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:17.160
In there, there is
a readme file that

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:20.250
explains the steps
necessary to expose

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:23.760
this service as an Apigee API.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.720
Namely, we need to provide a
couple of API configuration

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:32.190
data and also tag
the repository.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.400
That's all the developer needs
to know in order to expose

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:36.780
the service as an API.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:44.180
The developer decides
to go ahead and do this.

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.860
So once again, we switch
to the local editor

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:51.430
and edit the
configuration for the API.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:54.250
We need to provide a proxy
base, [? but ?] this is where

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:56.920
the API will be reachable.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:59.350
And we also need to
provide the back-end

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:02.815
it will be connected to, in our
case, our Hello World service.

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:15.500
Once again, the developer
commits their changes,

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:37.280
pushes the changes to the remote
repository, creates a new tag,

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:40.670
and finally pushes the
changes, including the new tag,

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:42.680
to the remote repository.

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:49.130
Once again, this
triggers another pipeline

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:50.840
that's managed by Cloud Build.

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:56.960
This pipeline, in
its first step,

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.960
generates an Apigee API
proxy based on a template.

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:07.030
It then deploys this API
into the Apigee environment

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:10.600
and also generates all
the associated artifacts

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:13.210
required for the API to run.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:16.900
It also shows an API
key that is required

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:18.655
in order to test this API.

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:26.590
The next step tests that the API
has been successfully deployed.

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:30.750
It [? simply ?] invokes a
couple of very simple requests,

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:34.760
one without an API key, and
the other one with an API key.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:44.460
The next few steps check the
code generated for the API back

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:47.640
into the source code repository.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.880
It is worth mentioning that
the API that's generated

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.130
incorporates all
the best practices

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:57.190
that a centralized API
team deems as necessary.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:00.750
For instance, verifying API
keys, checking for threats,

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:02.100
et cetera.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:04.680
Typically, this
centralized API team

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.950
will be in charge of managing
the templates used and will

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.790
continually update them
with best practices

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:14.640
as their security
posture changes.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.730
Let's now take a
look under the hood

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.230
and explore what the API does.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:24.020
For that, we will switch to
the Apigee user interface.

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.110
When we look at the API
proxies that have been defined,

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:30.250
we can see that now we
have a new one related

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:31.330
to our Hello service.

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:36.970
When we look into the
details of the proxy,

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:40.690
we can see that it is connected
to our Hello service, which

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:44.730
is available on the Hello
endpoint in the Anthos cluster

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:46.400
ingress.

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.460
Let's have a look at
the policies implemented

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:50.710
by this proxy.

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:53.860
Remember that these have
all been auto generated.

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:58.150
So first, it will add a
correlation ID to the request

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:00.940
if one doesn't exist.

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:04.890
If one exists, it'll just
make sure to remember it

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:08.610
so that it can inject
it back in the response.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.580
It'll then apply some
threat protection policies

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:14.230
to ensure that no attacks
are sent, in the headers,

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:15.760
for instance.

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:19.890
It will then verify that
an API key is provided

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:21.880
and that it is valid.

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:25.710
It will then enforce quota
limits on the number of calls,

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:30.590
and finally will apply JSON
threat protection policies

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:33.720
on the payload of the API.

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:36.310
Before the checks
pass successfully,

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:41.560
it will forward the request
to our Hello World Service.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:45.080
On the way back, it will
inject a correlation ID

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.590
into the Hello World
Service [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:49.600
preserve the one that
was sent in the request.

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:55.820
Let's now see the API in action.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:59.720
In order to do that, we will
start a very handy feature

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:03.650
that Apigee has that allows
you to trace every request that

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:06.530
comes into the API proxy.

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:09.170
For that, we'll use some
pre-prepared requests

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:10.910
that we have.

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:16.840
The first one sends a
request without an API key.

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:20.860
As expected, we get a 401
unauthorized response,

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:24.075
because there was no API
key included in the request.

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:31.370
Our second test doesn't
include an API key either,

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:35.660
but it does include
a malicious header.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:36.890
Let's test it out.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:45.920
As expected, we get an error,
because the API has detected

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:49.780
a threat in one of the headers.

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:53.270
Our next test, it's
a valid request.

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:56.360
It is a request that
does include an API key.

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:03.260
As expected, we get a
successful response.

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:05.850
And by the way, this
is the new version

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:11.380
of the service that was deployed
at the beginning of the demo.

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:14.530
It also includes
a correlation ID

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:17.770
that was automatically generated
because our request didn't

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:20.170
include one.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:26.050
Our next test does include a
correlation ID in the request.

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:29.890
When we execute it, we
can see that the response

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:32.940
does include that
correlation ID played back.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:42.140
Let's now send a
few more requests.

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:45.240
If we send them in
quick succession,

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:48.810
we will, at some point,
hit our quota limit.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:51.620
And that's when the API
will return an error.

00:15:57.756 --> 00:16:02.340
Let's see how Apigee handled
all of these requests.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:05.510
The first one is the
one that returned a 401.

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:08.270
That's when we didn't
have an API key.

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:12.480
The verify API key policy
failed in this case

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:15.150
and stopped any
further processing.

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:19.010
The second case, it's when
the threat protections failed.

00:16:19.010 --> 00:16:22.590
Again, [? all ?] for the
processing is stopped.

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:26.580
The next few requests are the
ones that were successful.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:28.410
We can see that
all of the policies

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:31.800
were successfully executed
and then their request

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:34.440
was sent to the target server.

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:36.670
When their response
was received,

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:41.550
it was forwarded back to
the client invoking the API,

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:46.050
including the newly
generated correlation ID.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:48.450
The last request,
it's the one that

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:51.940
failed because we had
exceeded our quota limit.

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:54.480
And, again, any further
processing was stopped.

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:00.380
In this demo, we have seen
modern software development

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:02.390
practices that are
commonly adopted

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:04.369
by the developer
community when building

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:07.010
services, such as CI/CD.

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:09.470
And we have also seen
how we can leverage

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:12.920
these practices to easily
solve the problem of exposing

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:16.730
these services to external
consumers in a secure way.

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:20.300
The service developer does not
need to be an API guru in order

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:23.240
to build APIs that incorporate
all of the required

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:25.050
best practices.

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:26.849
I hope you have
enjoyed the demo.

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:28.972
Thank you for watching it.

00:17:28.972 --> 00:17:30.180
GREG KUELGEN: Thanks, Debora.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:31.222
That all made good sense.

00:17:31.222 --> 00:17:34.110
And I think it's something
even I could understand.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:35.680
But I have a question.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:37.440
When do you generally
see customers

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:40.325
set something like this up?

00:17:40.325 --> 00:17:41.950
DEBORA ELKIN: Well,
generally customers

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:45.070
with larger enterprises
and mature programs

00:17:45.070 --> 00:17:47.760
are the ones who have set
this up in order to enable

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:50.170
a truly federated model.

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:53.470
By that I mean a model in
which API creation is not

00:17:53.470 --> 00:17:57.010
concentrated in a small
team of API experts,

00:17:57.010 --> 00:18:02.540
but rather distributed all
along the developer community.

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:03.772
GREG KUELGEN: OK, got it.

00:18:03.772 --> 00:18:05.480
But we all know the
security requirements

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:08.360
are going to evolve,
sometimes quite quickly.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:10.400
How do teams manage the
security of their APIs

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:12.887
across these distributed areas?

00:18:12.887 --> 00:18:14.470
DEBORA ELKIN: That's
when we generally

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:17.260
see a centralized team
that is responsible,

00:18:17.260 --> 00:18:20.650
not just for the creation of
these automated templates,

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:23.650
but also of the shared
flows that they use.

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:26.980
With a shared flow, you can
build reusable units of logic

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:29.320
that are invoked by the APIs.

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.800
In mature organizations,
security measures

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:35.740
are normally implemented
using shared flows.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:37.660
If the centralized
team sees the need

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:40.300
to update some of these
security measures,

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:42.300
they will update
the shared flow.

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:44.830
All APIs making use
of this shared flow

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:48.400
will automatically apply the
latest and greatest security

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:50.325
measures.

00:18:50.325 --> 00:18:53.860
GREG KUELGEN: Yeah, I
think that's a good plan.

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:56.320
So with that, I think
it's time to wrap up.

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:59.490
Today we talked about how you
can use Anthos an Apigee hybrid

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:01.800
together to secure APIs.

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:03.270
With Debora's
demo, you could see

00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:06.480
how easy it was for a developer
to expose an API using

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:09.360
best practices set up for them
by an API Center for Enablement

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:12.330
or similar team, allowing
the developer to concentrate

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:14.775
on delivering business value.

00:19:14.775 --> 00:19:16.650
I want to thank you for
watching this session

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:18.600
and offer to continue
the conversation.

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:21.900
Both Debora and I have set up
some expert one-on-one sessions

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:23.380
over the next couple of weeks.

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:25.550
We will also be
answering your questions

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:28.730
in the question and answer
session for this session.

